WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:08.960
At this very moment, in the depths of Earth’s
oceans, alien lifeforms are waiting. Not alien

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.790
in the sense they come from another planet,
but alien in the sense that they’re so different

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:19.790
from what we think of as life, they seem not
of this Earth. And if our oceans harbor organisms

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:26.990
that appear extraterrestrial, imagine what
we’d find in a literal alien sea. Polinices

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:33.440
is a speculative biology project by the brilliant
artist and worldbuilder J. J. Aniorte, which

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.079
explores life on a planet ruled by aquatic
organisms. The infinite forms of this tropical

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:43.989
paradise are among the most creative I’ve
ever featured on this channel — pushing

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:49.239
the boundaries of life as we know it. There’s
a tremendous amount to discover, and as always

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:53.899
you can follow and support the artist using
the links in the description. So, let’s

00:00:53.899 --> 00:01:02.579
dive in to these teeming waters, and see what
awaits us upon planet Polinices. The planet

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:08.970
of Polinices is dominated by a global ocean
that surrounds the planet’s sole supercontinent.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:15.040
Conditions on land are harsh, due to solar
radiation and brutal aridity. Yet just below

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:22.740
the waves, life is overflowing… Beginning
in the tropical shallows, we can find the

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:28.130
Stegocaudate, one of the most emblematic groups
of Polinices. At a glance, we seem to have

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:33.440
found the fish of this world. Yet Stegocaudate
— like all lifeforms we’ll discover on

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.660
this planet — are actually something far
stranger: colonial organisms. On Earth, colonial

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:46.360
organisms are lifeforms that are in fact made
of many different individual organisms working

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:52.660
in tandem to survive. Like organs in our bodies,
different individual organisms — which are

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:59.640
called ‘zooids’ — have different functions:
from locomotion, to digesting food, to reproducing.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.590
And the zooids that make up the Stegocaudate
are no different, with each individual organism

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:11.311
playing a role in the survival of the larger
lifeform. In order to understand this unique

00:02:11.311 --> 00:02:18.060
way of life, we must follow a Stegocaudate
from its birth to its death. As larvae, Stegocaudate

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.780
are transparent, and lack the distinctive
body shape of adults. As they grow older,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.330
their shell — which is at first covered
by a thin layer of tissue — becomes more

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:34.020
and more prominent as the tissue fades away.
The end result is a highly unusual lifeform

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:40.350
with exposed digestive and reproductive zooids
enclosed within a fish-shaped shell. While

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.610
an alien organism taking on a shape similar
to Earth fish might be surprising, the laws

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:52.520
of hydrodynamics have shaped non-fish animals
into fish-like forms before in our own oceans.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.810
This was the case for the phylliroe, a genus
of marine molluscs with a body plan and style

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:04.280
of locomotion almost identical to fish. The
shells of Stegocaudate are exceptionally light,

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:09.720
allowing them to swim freely. They can also
extend digestive zooid out of their shell

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.069
to vacuum up debris that gets entangled in
the cavities of their body. A fascinating

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:21.840
lifeform indeed. After the death of a stegocaudate,
its light, hollow shell usually reaches the

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.990
coast. With the digestive zooids faded away,
this skeletal frame has washed up on the shore

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:35.510
of the supercontinent. As on our own planet,
death is a central part of life on Polinices…

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:41.160
Yet while they live, the stegocaudate thrive,
often taking on the kinds of bright hues we

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:47.080
associate with tropical fish on earth. In
particular, they can be found in shallow reefs,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:52.599
which extend for hundreds of miles across
the sea floor in certain areas. These reefs

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:58.990
are made up primarily of hive sponges — an
unassuming yet highly unusual lifeform we’ll

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:04.379
discuss shortly. Before we do, there’s a
lifeform that clings to the reefs and demands

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:10.730
our attention. This maze-like organism is
a type of Onirocolia, a taxon closely related

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:16.720
to the stegocaudates. Their digestive zooids
grow in far more sinuous and intricate forms

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:23.300
than their relatives, creating patterns that
are completely surreal to look at. Most Onirocolia

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:29.740
remain stationary throughout their life, relying
on filter feeding for sustenance. Some genuses,

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.300
however, take to the seas like their stegocaudate
relatives — able to control their buoyancy

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:42.130
thanks to internal gas bladders. These labyrinthine
organisms float along like balloons, and can

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:48.780
feed on a wider variety of lifeforms. A far
more fish-like classification of colonial

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:55.090
organism can be found in the common Amphinatans.
These intriguing lifeforms don’t possess

00:04:55.090 --> 00:05:00.330
visible shells, and swim in small schools
similar in composition to many schools of

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:05.560
fish on Earth. Yet while Amphinatans might
look like fish at first glance, breaking down

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.940
their biology reveals they’re actually quite
distinct. Their eyes are located in the center

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.960
of their body, with two on each side. Evolved
from ancient digestive zooids, they give the

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:24.350
Amphinatans 360-degree vision. Furthermore,
their hidden mouth is located under these

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:30.400
eyes, and is connected by the folds of the
mantle to two siphons on their front and back.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.340
These siphons can suck in food and release
water for a jet-propelled escape. Across the

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.930
coastal reefs, different Amphinatans have
distinctive diets. The hive sponge eaters

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:48.510
are a notable group that feed almost exclusively
on the soft tissue of aquatic hive sponges.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:54.370
Due to the narrowness of their siphons, they’ve
lost the ability to use them as a jet propulsion

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:59.199
organ, although the siphons still possess
a tremendously powerful suction force to aid

00:05:59.199 --> 00:06:05.080
in consumption. Their patterned skin breaks
their silhouette on the reefs, a type of camouflage

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:11.080
convergent with many types of earth fish.
And when an Amphinatan is spotted by a predator,

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:16.030
they can take off in either direction. Since
their bodies are symmetrical, they can swim

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:21.160
forwards and backwards easily, retracting
one pair of their fins and using the others

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:27.590
to propel themselves along. And there is one
region in particular where Amphinatans thrive.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:33.800
The barrier reef of Kýanos Metrópoli is
the largest reef system of Polinices, extending

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:42.830
over 11,000 miles, or 18,000 km. Here, hive
sponges reach a breathtaking level of size,

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:48.380
color, and diversity. Some of these colonial
colonies are almost 10,000 years old, and

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:54.450
their nests can span several hundred square
meters. As a result, for the Amphinatan sponge

00:06:54.450 --> 00:07:00.440
eater, Kýanos Metrópoli is practically paradise.
Here, the Amphinatans have a veritable buffet

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:06.380
of sponges that extends in every direction:
a seemingly limitless source of potential

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:13.190
food. Yet as we’ll soon discover, the sponges
aren’t entirely defenseless...The hive sponges

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:18.340
themselves might be completely stationary.
When menaced by a sponge eating predator,

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:24.000
however, most types of hive sponges have an
unexpected defense. They can release swarms

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.669
of tiny lifeforms called drones into the water,
which descend upon predators to defend their

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:36.389
home. Under magnification, it becomes clear
that the tiny drones possess sharp mouthparts,

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:42.020
which can irritate predators that swim too
close to the hive. In many respects, the aquatic

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:48.440
drones have converged on a behavior similar
to that of Earth bees. Not only do they defend

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.770
their colonies with stinging strikes, but
they also collect food for the hive sponges

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:59.120
in the form of planktonic organisms. Yet perhaps
the most incredible aspect of the hive is

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:05.020
that it communicates with its drones through
radio wave frequencies. Specialized structures,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:10.630
which are actually highly derived drones themselves,
serve as signal repeaters to transfer signals

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:17.030
from the hive to drones that travel long distances
in search of food. As incredible as this likely

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:22.061
sounds, it’s possible that brittle stars
— a close relative of the starfish, can

00:08:22.061 --> 00:08:27.520
detect radio waves and might even use them
to navigate their surroundings. Although studies

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.719
are ongoing, it seems this is possible thanks
to specialized structures along their cell

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:39.580
membrane that function like tiny radio antennae.
So, the radio wave communication of hive sponges

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:46.110
— while unexpected — is far from impossible.
Radio waves don’t travel far underwater,

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:51.300
so a wide variety of biological receivers
have emerged to aid in getting signals to

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:58.220
the drones. Yet other lifeforms on Polinices
have taken radio communication to even stranger

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:04.640
levels. In the larger taxon of radioscriptorans,
the ‘Chain Fish’ are organisms unlike

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.149
any other. These lifeforms are characterized
by having the body divided into three or more

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:16.080
modules that swim in an orderly line. While
they appear to be unique individuals, they

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:21.480
share the same genetic information and cannot
live without each other. Therefore — although

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.810
they are not linked by any type of tissue,
they are considered a single organism. As

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:32.709
stated before, colonial organisms in our oceans
are indeed made up of many different living

00:09:32.709 --> 00:09:37.990
units working together, although they’re
typically connected by some kind of tissue.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:44.560
But for the various types of chain fish, connectivity
comes once again in the form of radio waves.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.250
These bizarre processions communicate and
sense each other’s position entirely through

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:55.470
short range frequencies. And so, the Chain
Fish move along the seabed, continuing their

00:09:55.470 --> 00:10:01.630
bizarre way of life.Throughout the global
ocean, perhaps the ultimate realization of

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:08.060
radio communication comes in the form of a
larger network that has emerged between individual

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:14.110
sponges. Using complex frequencies, hive sponges
can send signals to each other, including

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:19.269
information about their environment, calls
for help, or even stimuli like pain, that

00:10:19.269 --> 00:10:25.520
other individuals can experience as their
own. This intercommunication has, over millions

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:31.870
of years, led to a complex evolutionary arms
race — producing adaptations like the encryption

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:38.060
of messages, ‘passwords,’ and even the
sending of deceptive signals. This biological

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:43.790
internet is an incredible feat of evolution,
and something unique to planet Polinices.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:51.010
And speaking of unique, another unprecedented
lifeform exists in the form of dipterognathes.

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:56.350
As larvae, these lifeforms seem to have two
heads, but their mouth is actually located

00:10:56.350 --> 00:11:02.320
in the middle of their bodies, which is connected
to two independent stomachs located on opposite

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:07.890
ends of their figure. As they get older, these
twin digestive systems drag out behind the

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:13.370
‘head’ like tails, connected to the body
by long pharyngeal ducts. This strange body

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:19.180
plan arose from the fusion of two digestive
zooids — but today, all three regions are

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:25.670
controlled by a single brain. In coastal lagoons,
warm and calm waters provide ideal locations

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:32.519
for the Dipteroganthes to flourish. This particular
specimen is an Echinocephalus, characterized

00:11:32.519 --> 00:11:38.000
by pharyngeal ducts that can reach almost
1 and a half feet, or half a meter in length.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:44.130
While everything about the Dipteroganthes
is somewhat bizarre, they are a highly successful,

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:49.640
albeit highly specialized, form of life in
this region. This environment is also home

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:56.241
to distinct varieties of hive sponges, which
form impressive reefs on the seafloor. And

00:11:56.241 --> 00:12:02.370
nestled among reefs like this one, a bizarre
filter feeder is waiting. This is a ‘Sea

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:08.110
Belt,’ a distant relative of the hive sponge.
This organism is formed from a stationary

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:13.630
hive and hundreds of drones joined together
by suction cups, forming a structure similar

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:18.740
to a circular ribbon. The belt constantly
rotates so that each drone passes through

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:24.520
the interior of the hive and deposits plankton
trapped during the spin. The living hive then

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:30.150
digests this plankton and transforms it into
a ‘nectar’ that feeds the drones. Truly,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:36.860
there’s no other organism like it. Other
organisms get around by very different means.

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:42.540
Rotigrada is a diverse class of lifeforms
that traverse the seabed by rotating themselves

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:48.120
via contractions of their muscles. This form
of locomotion has earned them the common name

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:54.279
of ‘Sea Wheels.’ Each individual zooid
in the larger wheel possesses two legs, which

00:12:54.279 --> 00:13:00.550
look a bit like the spindly legs of Earth
millipedes. Unlike millipedes, however, Sea

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:05.579
Wheels have no true front or back, with every
zooid on the wheel having their own simple

00:13:05.579 --> 00:13:11.230
mouth. As a result, which mouth gets used
depends entirely on which section of the Sea

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:17.000
Wheel is closest to their prey on a given
hunt. And so, the wheel spins on, feeding

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:22.990
upon whatever lies in its path. Along the
rocky shore of the supercontinent, we can

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:29.010
find something exceedingly rare for planet
Polinices — a creature that can move upon

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:34.950
dry land. At least, to some degree. This massive
lifeform is a colony of carnivorous pharyngopods,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:39.880
which work together to drag the serpentine
body of the colony out of the water to catch

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:45.660
prey in puddles along the shoreline. A pair
of simple eyes located at each of its ends

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:51.779
provides simple visual information that facilitates
navigation in this environment. The flexible

00:13:51.779 --> 00:13:58.250
appendages that the colony uses to get around
resemble the tube feet of Sea Pigs, an unusual

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:04.251
type of Sea Cucumber that walk along the floor
of our oceans on tube-shaped legs. Yet the

00:14:04.251 --> 00:14:09.500
thousands of tentacular appendages on the
underside of the intertidal colony aren’t

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:15.050
just feet — but are actually individual
zooids, with mouths, teeth, and stomachs.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:21.480
And at almost ten feet, this shambling goliath
is one of the largest and most dangerous predators

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:28.420
in its environment. And at last, the final
lifeform on planet Polinices we’ll be discussing

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:34.920
is the Laimargia — one of the most recognizable
organisms in the Polinices reefs. This bottom

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:40.139
feeding lifeform is adapted to engulf sand
and digest the organic matter found in it

00:14:40.139 --> 00:14:46.030
— playing a very important role in the ecosystem
by recycling nutrients. The most impressive

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:51.959
characteristic of Laimargia, however, is its
size. Some species of the genus can reach

00:14:51.959 --> 00:14:59.260
almost 200 feet, or 60 meters in length, making
them without a doubt the most massive polizoans.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:05.019
As incredible as this sounds, some of the
absolute largest Earth Siphonophore colonies

00:15:05.019 --> 00:15:10.430
do reach similar lengths in the deep sea — although
they can’t quite match the Laimargia’s

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:15.930
size. In possessing such incredible bulk,
the enormous bodies of Laimargia serve as

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:22.220
a habitat for a plethora of organisms — who
find refuge upon its trunk. As a result, the

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:29.740
Laimargia resemble a moving landscape steadily
shifting across the sea floor. The waters

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:34.910
of Polinices are home to some of the most
surreal forms of life I’ve ever documented

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:40.850
for the archive. Even the most alien organisms
in our oceans aren’t quite as surreal as

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:47.440
what swims below those waves. If you enjoyed
learning about the complex creatures of Polinices,

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:52.190
you can follow and support the artist using
the links in the description. An artbook exploring

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:56.899
more about this amazing planet will be coming
sometime in the future — so keep an eye

00:15:56.899 --> 00:16:03.350
out! And as always, thanks for watching. If
you enjoyed this entry, please lend your support

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:09.449
and like, subscribe, and hit the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:16:09.449 --> 00:16:30.850
See you in the next video.

